There are currently in existence certain rules and regulations adopted by the county board of health with the purpose of preserving the public health, safety, and welfare of citizens of the county. This chapter specifically incorporates by reference the provision of the following rules and regulations which are in force at the time of the adoption of this chapter and as amended:
(1) Rules and regulations pertaining to rabies control (adopted by the county board of health and being the county board of health rule II as subsequently amended and updated), and policies of the county board of health pertaining to rabies control;
(2) Rules and regulations for on-site sewage management systems (adopted by the department of human resources (ch. 290-5-26-.01-.20) and the county board of health and being the county board of health rule III as subsequently amended and updated), and policies of the county board of health pertaining to on-site sewage management systems;
(3) Rules and regulations pertaining to on-site water supplies (adopted by the county board of health and being the county board of health rule IV as subsequently amended and updated), and policies of the county board of health pertaining to on-site water supplies;
(4) Rules and regulations pertaining to food service (adopted by the department of human resources (ch. 290-5-14) and the county board of health and being the county board of health rule V as subsequently amended and updated), and policies of the county board of health pertaining to food service;
(5) Rules and regulations pertaining to public swimming pools, spas, and recreational water parks (adopted by the department of human resources (ch. 290-5-57) and the county board of health and being the county board of health rule VI as subsequently amended and updated), and policies of the county board of health pertaining to public swimming pools, spas, and recreational water parks;
(6) Rules and regulations pertaining to tourist accommodations (adopted by the department of human resources (ch. 290-5-18) and the county board of health and being the county board of health rule VI as subsequently amended and updated), and policies of the county board of health pertaining to tourist accommodations;
(7) Rules and regulations pertaining to body art (adopted by the county board of health and being the county board of health rule VIII as subsequently amended and updated), and policies of the county board of health pertaining to body art.
(Ord. of 10-9-1989, § I)

270:
It shall be unlawful for any person within the unincorporated areas of the county to engage in any conduct described in the following subsections:
(1) To act in a violent or tumultuous manner toward another whereby any person is placed in fear of the safety of his life, limb, or health;
(2) To act in a violent or tumultuous manner toward another whereby the property of any person is placed in danger of being damaged or destroyed;
(3) To cause, provoke or engage in any fight, brawl or riotous conduct so as to endanger the life, limb, health, or property of another;
(4) To assemble or congregate with another for the purpose of or with the intent to engage in gaming;
(5) To be in or about any place, along or with another with the purpose of or intent to engage in any fraudulent scheme, trick or device to obtain any money or valuable thing; or to aid or abet any person or persons in doing so;
(6) To be in or about any place where gaming or the illegal sale or possession of alcoholic beverages or narcotics or dangerous drugs (controlled substances) is practiced, allowed or tolerated, for the purpose of or intent to engage in gaming or the purchase, use, possession or consumption of such illegal drugs, narcotics or alcoholic beverages;
(7) To direct fighting words toward another, that is, words which by their very nature tend to incite a breach of the peace;
(8) To interfere, by acts of physical obstruction, with another's pursuit of a lawful occupation;
(9) To congregate with another in or on any public way or grounds so as to halt the flow of vehicular or pedestrian traffic, and to fail to clear that public way or grounds after being ordered to do so by a county law officer or other lawful authority;
(10) To disrupt, by actions which tend to incite a breach of peace, the activities of any house of worship, hospital, home for the elderly or any gathering, or procession;
(11) To throw bottles, paper, cans, glass, sticks, stones, missiles, or any other debris on public property; or
(12) To be and appear in an intoxicated condition in any public place or within the curtilage of any private residence not their own other than by invitation of the owner or lawful occupant, which condition is made manifest by boisterousness, by indecent condition or act, or by vulgar, profane, or loud unbecoming language.
(Ord. of 12-11-1995, § A)

279:
(a) Should a property owner desire that a new road be developed on his property for public use that is to be maintained by the county, the property owner must:
(1) Make application to the board of commissioners showing location of road;
(2) Clear and prepare road right-of-way to satisfaction of board;
(3) Furnish fill dirt as may be requested by the board;
(4) Deed right-of-way to the county with provision that should the county ever cease to maintain road for public use, that said right-of-way will revert to landowner;
(5) Furnish all pipes, save one, that are required in the construction of the road.
(b) The county reserves the right to accept rights-of-way or to reject proposals for the county to maintain roads.
(Res. of 3-11-1985)

301:
(a) The board of commissioners does hereby authorize the county tax commissioner, pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 48-5-242 and any other applicable law, to waive penalties and/or interest on unpaid ad valorem taxes under the following conditions:
(1) Notice to the taxpayer was not sufficient, such as in the case of a new purchaser that was not reflected in the tax commissioner's files or an address error in the tax file;
(2) Dire illness of the taxpayer that resulted in the taxpayer being hospitalized, in a nursing or rehabilitative home, or otherwise not present in the home for an extended period of time;
(3) Where the penalty and interest is less than $5.00;
(4) Taxpayer has provided substantial evidence of assessment error.
(b) The waiver for the reasons listed in subsection (a) of this section may be issued when the tax commissioner reasonably determines that the default giving rise to the penalty and/or interest to be waived was due to reasonable cause and not due to gross negligence or willful neglect or disregard of the law or of regulations or instructions issued pursuant to the law and when the interest to be waived accrues.
(Res. of 6-13-2011(2); Res. of 2-11-2013)

